e": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "meta": {"retrieved": ["K8S_RUNBOOK#first-three-commands-for-any-failing-pod", "K8S_RUNBOOK#kubernetes-troubleshooting-runbook-crashloopbackoff-and-oomk", "K8S_RUNBOOK#oomkilled", "K8S_RUNBOOK#after-the-fix", "README#rolling-upgrades-releasing-under-load-p2"], "cited": ["K8S_RUNBOOK#first-three-commands-for-any-failing-pod"], "expected": ["K8S_RUNBOOK#first-three-commands-for-any-failing-pod"], "answer": "Running `kubectl logs` without `--previous` shows the new container, which often has not failed yet. The log explaining why the last container died is only available with `--previous`, and in `kubectl describe pod` under **Last State**. The runbook lists it as one of the first three commands for any failing pod, e.g. `kubectl --context kind-qa -n qa logs <pod> --previous`."}}
{"prompt_id": "oom-vs-eviction", "rep": 0, "prompt": "How is OOMKilled different from an eviction?", "tags": ["k8s-runbook", "answerable", "drafted"], "status": "ok", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "grade": {"correct": 1.0, "grounded": 1.0, "abstain_ok": 1.0, "retrieval_hit": 1.0, "cited_ok": 1.0}, "explanation": {"correct": "The answer covers every key fact in the reference: the container exceeds its memory limit, the kernel kills it with exit 137 and it restarts, while under node memory pressure the kubelet evicts whole pods that show status Evicted. Every claim, including the extras about SIGKILL, CrashLoopBackOff and Guaranteed QoS being evicted last, appears in the excerpts."}, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "fallback_credit": null, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2904, "iterations": [{"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 2904, "model": null, "output_tokens": 221, "type": "message"}], "output_tokens": 221, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "latency_s": 3.16, "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "judge_usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "fallback_credit": null, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 3217, "iterations": [{"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 3217, "model": null, "output_tokens": 148, "type": "message"}], "output_tokens": 148, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "meta": {"retrieved": ["K8S_RUNBOOK#oomkilled", "K8S_RUNBOOK#after-the-fix", "K8S_RUNBOOK#crashloopbackoff", "K8S_RUNBOOK#kubernetes-troubleshooting-runbook-crashloopbackoff-and-oomk", "K8S_RUNBOOK#first-three-commands-for-any-failing-pod"], "cited": ["K8S_RUNBOOK#oomkilled"], "expected": ["K8S_RUNBOOK#oomkilled"], "answer": "OOMKilled means a single container used more memory than its memory limit and the kernel killed it at once with SIGKILL. Kubernetes records Reason `OOMKilled` and exit code 137, then restarts the container (repeated kills lead to CrashLoopBackOff). An eviction happens when the node itself is under memory pressure: the kubelet evicts whole pods, which then show status `Evicted`, not `OOMKilled`. Pods in the Guaranteed QoS class (memory request equal to limit) are evicted last under node pressure."}}
