res-in-docker", "README#the-system-under-test", "README#the-database-postgres-in-docker-p2", "README#or-in-docker-nothing-to-install-but-docker", "README#project-layout"], "cited": ["README#the-database-postgres-in-docker"], "expected": ["README#the-database-postgres-in-docker"], "answer": "The data is stored in memory (tmpfs) on purpose. Every `docker compose down` discards it, so the next run starts from an empty database, and memory is also faster than disk. A real service would instead keep the data on a named volume (`volumes: [db-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data]`), which survives `down` and is deleted only by `down -v`."}}
{"prompt_id": "db-tmpfs", "rep": 1, "prompt": "Why is the Postgres data kept in memory (tmpfs)?", "tags": ["docker-db", "answerable", "drafted"], "status": "ok", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "grade": {"correct": 1.0, "grounded": 1.0, "abstain_ok": 1.0, "retrieval_hit": 1.0, "cited_ok": 1.0}, "explanation": {"correct": "The answer states both reasons (data discarded on each `docker compose down` so the next run starts empty, and memory is faster) and notes a real service would use a named volume. All of this, including the volume syntax and the `down -v` detail, is stated in the README's 'Where the data lives' paragraph."}, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "fallback_credit": null, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4264, "iterations": [{"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 4264, "model": null, "output_tokens": 164, "type": "message"}], "output_tokens": 164, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "latency_s": 2.55, "judge_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "judge_usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "fallback_credit": null, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4492, "iterations": [{"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 4492, "model": null, "output_tokens": 116, "type": "message"}], "output_tokens": 116, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "meta": {"retrieved": ["README#the-database-postgres-in-docker", "README#the-system-under-test", "README#the-database-postgres-in-docker-p2", "README#or-in-docker-nothing-to-install-but-docker", "README#project-layout"], "cited": ["README#the-database-postgres-in-docker"], "expected": ["README#the-database-postgres-in-docker"], "answer": "It is kept in memory (tmpfs) on purpose for two reasons. First, each `docker compose down` throws the data away, so the next run starts from an empty database. Second, memory is faster than disk. A real service would instead keep the data on a named volume (`volumes: [db-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data]`), which survives `down` and is deleted only by `down -v`."}}
