ard", "speed": "standard"}, "meta": {"retrieved": ["README#performance-testing", "README#the-system-under-test", "README#design-decisions", "README#design-decisions-p2", "TEST_PLAN#scenarios"], "cited": ["README#performance-testing"], "expected": ["README#performance-testing"], "answer": "Running 200 logins as a single user mostly measures a cache, and in a real system it would hit per-user rate limits or account lockout. The \"login, different users\" scenario gives each of the 200 users its own API key and also checks that each token belongs to its user. A token can read its own user's transactions (200) and is refused the next user's (403). The `login_users` fixture uses the admin key to create the users and their keys (`POST /api/users/:id/api-keys`) before timing starts, and deletes them afterwards."}}
